WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.750
On the Holderness coast of East Yorkshire,

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.330
26 villages listed in the Domesday
Book have been lost to the sea.

3
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.170
Its cliffs slump into the waves.

4
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.390
In Dorset, white chalk at Handfast
marks the edge of Swanage Bay.

5
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:16.950
Rivers differ too.

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.050
On 5 December 2015, Storm Desmond gave
the Tees its highest recorded flow.

7
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.110
The Lambourn, a chalk stream,
runs clear and steady.

8
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.670
So why do some coasts and rivers
change many times faster than others?

9
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.470
The rock?

10
00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.350
The waves?

11
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.950
What people build?

12
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.210
In this mission, you'll
compare two coasts,

13
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.490
and two rivers through the year.

14
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.770
Then you'll see what storms,
floods and sea defences can do.

15
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.670
Your mission: explain why some landscapes
change so much faster than others.
